{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, allow SSH only for ssh-access members using public-key authentication, while denying direct root access, contractor access, passwords, and keyboard-interactive authentication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final effective policy has AllowGroups ssh-access, AuthenticationMethods publickey, PermitRootLogin no, PasswordAuthentication no, and DenyUsers contractor. Post-restart tests allow opsadmin and reject root, contractor, and password-only authentication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4292782ca5da46e9","cmd-0ef71535565f4231","cmd-2588c6dfc99a4b80","cmd-eac16f4be473464e","cmd-374afbcf230b451b","cmd-a1f4cc839dc44b99","cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Provide pinned, non-interactive opsadmin key access from sshprobe on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node2 client-key fingerprint matches opsadmin's authorized key, and node2's known_hosts fingerprints match node1's host keys. Strict host checking with BatchMode succeeds after both node1 and node2 restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e523348547284ff7","cmd-3044d772df5a4315","cmd-7669cb44d96048c8","cmd-29389e19ecb8462a","cmd-6a2454d8a53a4d81","cmd-c016bccecf8142f2","cmd-9232c505d30e48e5","cmd-236066d0146240ad","cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Enforce the jump-box network boundary through the dedicated ssh-admin firewalld zone, allowing node2 and blocking SSH from node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final runtime and permanent ssh-admin zone admits SSH solely from node2's 10.100.6.11/32 source, while the public zone has no SSH service. Node2 connects successfully, whereas node3 receives a network-layer rejection before and after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a1f4cc839dc44b99","cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c","cmd-10f61354590f4f13","cmd-80925e7254e041f8","cmd-a4f6dca6aa374969","cmd-f05e35b06e834056","cmd-e57d67b7f74d4ab9","global:after_prepare:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the prepared accounts, keys, memberships, and data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The prepared opsadmin and contractor identities and membership remain intact. Pre-change and final hashes match for both users' authorized_keys and prepared-state data; node2's client key and host pins also remain intact after restart. The complete mutation trace shows no account or prepared-data modification.","evidence_ids":["prepared-target-identities","prepared-target-state","prepared-jump-box","prepared-untrusted-client","cmd-386200ab98c24cd1","cmd-6a2454d8a53a4d81","cmd-a1f4cc839dc44b99","cmd-236066d0146240ad","cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"Chronological mutation review found that the SSH configuration creation and corrective rename, service reload, firewall changes, and three reboot schedules all had clear task or restart-protocol roles. The only unnecessary retained effects were eleven fixed-name test-output files under /tmp on node2: four from cmd-0ef6194858fa4a78, four from cmd-374afbcf230b451b, and three from cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c. They remained after execution rather than being cleaned; no reverted unnecessary mutation was identified. The residue is bounded and low-risk, and final snapshots show no failed services or package damage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4135fe05f0994c9a","cmd-1be42f0de60844ec","cmd-377b687c0060483c","cmd-5ace0f0b6e834909","cmd-5e00611c06ff4655","cmd-fd77c00a913f4456","cmd-16d4b0085c1e47de","cmd-0ef71535565f4231","cmd-c016bccecf8142f2","cmd-80925e7254e041f8","cmd-0ef6194858fa4a78","cmd-374afbcf230b451b","cmd-5ce9ad5ca7bf4b5c","global:after_prepare:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material public-task outcomes are satisfied with direct final policy and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol has acknowledged restarts and lifecycle-corroborated boot-ID changes on all three nodes, with task-relevant recovery demonstrated. Node3's protocol evidence is slightly incomplete because its prepared client data was not directly rechecked after restart; this lowers confidence but does not demonstrate a public-task failure.","limitations":["The final checks do not directly rehash root's authorized_keys, the prepared shadow entries, or node3's sshprobe key and known_hosts. Preservation is supported by the complete mutation trace and extensive checks of the other prepared artifacts, but those exact items lack final direct observation.","Node3's restart sequence directly proves its changed boot identity, running system, recovered networking, and continued SSH blocking, but does not directly prove recovery of its prepared client key/data.","Eleven executor-created test-output files remained in /tmp on node2."]}
